"""Real, publicly documented case studies. Every fact below was checked against the cited page on 2026-10-12.
Subjects without an entry keep their scenario, labelled 'Illustrative scenario'."""
def C(org,title,url,summary,lesson): return dict(org=org,title=title,url=url,summary=summary,lesson=lesson)
KLARNA=C("Klarna","Klarna AI assistant handles two-thirds of customer service chats in its first month (press release, Feb 2024)",
 "https://www.klarna.com/international/press/klarna-ai-assistant-handles-two-thirds-of-customer-service-chats-in-its-first-month/",
 "Klarna reported that its OpenAI-powered assistant had 2.3 million conversations in its first month, two-thirds of its customer service chats, doing work it equated to 700 full-time agents. It reported customer satisfaction on par with human agents, a 25% drop in repeat inquiries, and resolution in under 2 minutes versus 11 minutes before, in 23 markets and more than 35 languages.",
 "Product teams should define the outcome metrics up front (resolution time, repeat contacts, satisfaction) and report them against the human baseline. These are company-reported figures, so treat them as a claim to compare against, not an independent benchmark.")
UBERGW=C("Uber","Navigating the LLM Landscape: Uber's Innovation with GenAI Gateway (Uber Engineering, Jul 2024)","https://www.uber.com/blog/genai-gateway/",
 "Uber's Michelangelo team built a single GenAI Gateway, a Go service, for more than 60 LLM use cases. It mirrors the OpenAI HTTP/JSON interface while routing to external vendors and Uber-hosted models. It adds a PII redactor that anonymizes requests before they go to vendors and restores the entities afterwards, plus auth, metrics and audit logs for cost attribution. At the time of writing it served about 30 customer teams and 16 million queries per month.",
 "One OpenAI-compatible front door lets you add security review, PII handling, budgets and logging once, for every team. PII redaction has latency and quality costs that you need to measure.")
ANTHRES=C("Anthropic","How we built our multi-agent research system (Anthropic Engineering)","https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system",
 "Anthropic describes its Research feature: a lead agent plans and spawns parallel subagents that search, then synthesizes their findings. On its internal research eval, a Claude Opus 4 lead with Claude Sonnet 4 subagents outperformed single-agent Opus 4 by 90.2%. Multi-agent runs used about 15× the tokens of chat, and token usage alone explained 80% of performance variance on BrowseComp.",
 "Multi-agent designs buy breadth by spending tokens. Use them for high-value, parallelizable tasks.")
CONTEXTUAL=C("Anthropic","Introducing Contextual Retrieval (Sep 2024)","https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval",
 "Anthropic prepends a short, model-generated context to each chunk before embedding it and indexing it for BM25. In their experiments, contextual embeddings cut the top-20 retrieval failure rate by 35% (5.7% → 3.7%). Adding contextual BM25 brought it to 49% (→ 2.9%), and adding reranking to 67% (→ 1.9%).",
 "Many retrieval failures are chunks that lost their context. Fix the chunk and combine lexical plus semantic search with reranking before you reach for a bigger model.")
HONEY=C("Honeycomb","All the Hard Stuff Nobody Talks About when Building Products with LLMs","https://www.honeycomb.io/blog/hard-stuff-nobody-talks-about-llm",
 "Honeycomb shipped Query Assistant, which turns natural language into Honeycomb queries. The post explains that the prompt must carry domain knowledge, the user's actual schema (real column names), several few-shot examples and the existing query, all inside a limited context window. That forced hard choices about which columns to include for large schemas.",
 "Context engineering is product engineering. Decide what the model must see (schema, examples, current state) and budget the window for it.")
LINKEDIN=C("LinkedIn","Musings on Building a Generative AI Product (LinkedIn Engineering)","https://www.linkedin.com/blog/engineering/generative-ai/musings-on-building-a-generative-ai-product",
 "LinkedIn's team routes each question to a specialized agent and calls internal APIs as 'skills'. The LLM outputs the call parameters in structured form, and they chose YAML over JSON because it uses fewer tokens. They also inject retrieved response examples into prompts with embedding-based retrieval. They note that getting to 80% quality was fast, but the last 20% took most of the work.",
 "Structured tool calls need a compact format, schema-matching parameters and a plan for malformed output. Budget most of your effort for the long tail of quality.")
LISQL=C("LinkedIn","Practical Text-to-SQL for Data Analytics (LinkedIn Engineering)","https://www.linkedin.com/blog/engineering/ai/practical-text-to-sql-for-data-analytics",
 "LinkedIn built SQL Bot inside its DARWIN data science platform. It finds the right tables, writes and fixes queries, and runs them under the user's permissions. It builds a knowledge graph from DataHub (schemas, field descriptions, top values, partition keys, metric vs dimension classification). About 60% of benchmark questions turned out to have multiple valid answers, and ignoring that under-reported recall by 10–15%.",
 "Text-to-SQL quality comes from metadata (descriptions, semantics, examples) and honest evals that accept several correct answers.")
DATAHUB=C("LinkedIn / DataHub","DataHub: Popular metadata architectures explained","https://www.linkedin.com/blog/engineering/data-management/datahub-popular-metadata-architectures-explained",
 "The post describes three generations of data catalog architecture. Crawl-based catalogs pull metadata periodically and go stale. Service-based catalogs offer push APIs. Event-based catalogs stream metadata changes as a log. It explains why push and streaming keep metadata fresh and trustworthy at scale, which led to DataHub's design at LinkedIn.",
 "If agents will rely on the catalog at runtime, freshness matters. Prefer push or event-based metadata over nightly crawls.")
SHOPCDC=C("Shopify","Capturing Every Change From Shopify's Sharded Monolith (Shopify Engineering, 2021)","https://shopify.engineering/capturing-every-change-shopify-sharded-monolith",
 "Shopify replaced query-based batch extraction with log-based CDC using Debezium and Kafka Connect over the 100+ MySQL shards behind its core monolith. Debezium's default of one topic per table per shard did not fit, so the team had to design how topics map to tables across shards.",
 "Log-based CDC gives you every change without hammering the database. Plan topic and schema layout up front, especially for sharded sources.")
SIDEKICK=C("Shopify","Building production-ready agentic systems: Lessons from Shopify Sidekick (2025)","https://shopify.engineering/building-production-ready-agentic-systems",
 "Sidekick grew from a few tools to dozens. Shopify found tool boundaries blurred and the system prompt became unmanageable, so it moved to 'just-in-time instructions' returned alongside tool results. It calibrated specialized LLM judges against human annotators, treating human agreement as the ceiling. It later used GRPO training with those judges and ran into reward hacking.",
 "Keep tool sets small with clear boundaries, deliver guidance when a tool needs it, and calibrate judges against humans before trusting or training on them.")
DOORDASH=C("DoorDash","Path to high-quality LLM-based Dasher support automation (Sep 2024)","https://careersatdoordash.com/blog/large-language-modules-based-dasher-support-automation/",
 "DoorDash's RAG support bot is checked by a two-tier LLM Guardrail: first a cheap semantic-similarity check against knowledge-base segments, then an LLM evaluator for groundedness, coherence and compliance. They report it reduced hallucinations by 90% and severe compliance issues by 99%. An LLM Judge monitors five quality aspects, calibrated against a human review team, and new issues become promptfoo regression tests.",
 "Put a cheap check first and an LLM judge second, keep humans calibrating the judge, and turn found failures into regression tests.")
RECHAT=C("Rechat (via Hamel Husain)","Your AI Product Needs Evals: Case Study: Lucy, A Real Estate AI Assistant","https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/evals/",
 "Rechat's assistant Lucy improved quickly with prompt engineering, then plateaued. Fixing one failure caused another, quality was judged by 'vibe checks', and prompts grew unwieldy. The case study shows how they built unit-test-style assertions, trace logging and human plus model evaluation to make progress measurable again.",
 "When quality plateaus, stop tweaking prompts and build the eval system: look at traces, write assertions, and iterate against them.")
TESTGEN=C("Meta","Automated Unit Test Improvement using Large Language Models at Meta (arXiv 2402.09171)","https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.09171",
 "Meta's TestGen-LLM generates candidate unit tests and keeps only those that build, pass reliably and increase coverage. On Instagram Reels and Stories, 75% of generated tests built, 57% passed reliably and 25% increased coverage. At test-a-thons it improved 11.5% of the classes it was applied to, and engineers accepted 73% of its recommendations.",
 "Filter agent output through automatic, objective checks before any human sees it. That is what made LLM-written code acceptable at scale.")
GOOGMIG=C("Google","How is Google using AI for internal code migrations? (arXiv 2501.06972)","https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.06972",
 "Google reports on using LLMs, combined with conventional tooling, for large internal code migrations. They find evidence that this can significantly reduce the time needed for migrations and lower the barrier to starting and finishing them.",
 "Agents work best on long, repetitive, verifiable tasks, with existing build and test systems acting as the check.")
LLAMA3=C("Meta","The Llama 3 Herd of Models (arXiv 2407.21783), section on infrastructure reliability","https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.21783",
 "During a 54-day snapshot of Llama 3 pre-training, Meta saw 466 job interruptions: 47 planned and 419 unexpected. About 78% of the unexpected ones were attributed to confirmed or suspected hardware issues such as GPU failures. Even so, they achieved more than 90% effective training time with automated maintenance.",
 "At this scale, failure is routine. Fast detection, automated restarts and checkpointing decide how much compute you actually get.")
METACAP=C("Meta","Maintaining large-scale AI capacity at Meta (Engineering at Meta, Jun 2024)","https://engineering.fb.com/2024/06/12/production-engineering/maintaining-large-scale-ai-capacity-meta/",
 "Meta describes keeping GPU training clusters healthy while constantly upgrading them. That means more than 30 maintenance operations touching over 50 components, with many rollouts overlapping at any time, done without stalling training jobs.",
 "Training infrastructure needs maintenance designed in from the start: rolling, overlapping and coordinated with job scheduling.")
METAGENAI=C("Meta","Building Meta's GenAI Infrastructure (Engineering at Meta, Mar 2024)","https://engineering.fb.com/2024/03/12/data-center-engineering/building-metas-genai-infrastructure/",
 "Meta built two 24,576-GPU clusters for Llama 3, one on a RoCE fabric (Arista 7800 with OCP rack switches) and one on NVIDIA Quantum2 InfiniBand. This let them compare fabrics at scale. They stated a goal of 350,000 H100 GPUs by the end of 2024.",
 "Both RoCE and InfiniBand can run frontier training. Fabric choice is an engineering and supply trade-off, best settled with your own measurements.")
METAROCE=C("Meta","RoCE networks for distributed AI training at scale (Engineering at Meta, Aug 2024)","https://engineering.fb.com/2024/08/05/data-center-engineering/roce-network-distributed-ai-training-at-scale/",
 "Meta explains moving to a two-stage Clos topology for AI racks (an 'AI Zone'), with rack training switches as the leaf layer. It then covers the routing and congestion-control work needed to run RoCE for large training jobs.",
 "Collective performance is a property of topology plus routing plus congestion control. Design and debug them together.")
OPENAIK8S=C("OpenAI","Scaling Kubernetes to 7,500 nodes (Jan 2021)","https://openai.com/index/scaling-kubernetes-to-7500-nodes/",
 "OpenAI runs large ML jobs with typically one pod per node. Healthchecks cordon bad nodes, and 'preflight' GPU tests run behind a taint before a node takes work. Team capacity is enforced with taints and tolerations. All-or-nothing gang scheduling uses the Coscheduling plugin to avoid half-scheduled deadlocks. EndpointSlices cut a major API-server load by about 1000×.",
 "Accelerator clusters need health gating, gang scheduling and quota mechanisms that stock Kubernetes doesn't give you by default.")
APPLE=C("Apple","Introducing Apple's On-Device and Server Foundation Models (Apple ML Research, Jun 2024)","https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/introducing-apple-foundation-models",
 "Apple's ~3B-parameter on-device model uses mixed 2-bit and 4-bit palettization averaging 3.7 bits per weight, at the same accuracy as the uncompressed model, and can go to 3.5 bits without significant quality loss. Task-specific LoRA adapters (rank 16, tens of MB each) are loaded dynamically on top of one base model.",
 "On device, memory dictates the design. Compress the base model aggressively and specialize with small swappable adapters.")
MOONCAKE=C("Moonshot AI","Mooncake: A KVCache-centric Disaggregated Architecture for LLM Serving (arXiv 2407.00079)","https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.00079",
 "Mooncake, the serving platform for Moonshot AI's Kimi, separates prefill and decode clusters and pools KV cache across CPU, DRAM and SSD. It reports up to 525% more throughput in simulated long-context scenarios, and handling 75% more requests under real workloads while meeting SLOs.",
 "Disaggregation pays most for long prompts, and the KV cache becomes the resource you schedule around.")
ANYSCALE=C("Anyscale","How continuous batching enables 23x throughput in LLM inference while reducing p50 latency (Jun 2023)","https://www.anyscale.com/blog/continuous-batching-llm-inference",
 "Anyscale benchmarked static batching against continuous (iteration-level) batching in Hugging Face TGI and vLLM. In their benchmark, vLLM's continuous batching plus PagedAttention reached up to 23× the throughput of naive static batching while reducing median latency.",
 "Scheduling at the token level and managing KV memory well matter more than raw kernel speed for serving throughput. Benchmark on your own workloads.")
DEEPSEEK=C("DeepSeek","DeepSeek-V3 Technical Report (arXiv 2412.19437)","https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.19437",
 "DeepSeek-V3 is a Mixture-of-Experts model with 671B total parameters, of which 37B are active per token. It uses Multi-head Latent Attention and FP8 training. The report says full training took 2.788M H800 GPU hours.",
 "MoE separates total capacity from per-token compute. Memory still has to hold all experts while compute scales with active parameters.")
GRAB=C("Grab","LLM-powered data classification for data entities at scale (Grab Engineering)","https://engineering.grab.com/llm-powered-data-classification",
 "Grab uses LLMs to tag data entities (for example columns holding PII or other sensitive types) at scale, orchestrated by an internal service. That service is named Gemini, a name that predates Google's model.",
 "Automated classification makes privacy controls scalable, but verify the tags and route uncertain ones to data owners.")
JPM=C("JPMorgan Chase","How JPMorgan Chase built a data mesh architecture (AWS Big Data Blog)","https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-jpmorgan-chase-built-a-data-mesh-architecture-to-drive-significant-value-to-enhance-their-enterprise-data-platform/",
 "JPMC defines data products curated by the domain owners who understand the data. Each product's data sits in its own lake, made visible through a catalog (AWS Glue Data Catalog), shared under controls (Lake Formation) and queried in place (Athena).",
 "Data products with clear owners and controlled sharing let consumers, now including agents, use data without copying it everywhere.")
ZANZIBAR=C("Google","Zanzibar: Google's Consistent, Global Authorization System (USENIX ATC 2019)","https://research.google/pubs/zanzibar-googles-consistent-global-authorization-system/",
 "Zanzibar stores relationship-based ACLs for many Google services. It handles trillions of ACLs and millions of authorization checks per second, with 95th-percentile latency under 10 ms and more than 99.999% availability over three years. Its ordering guarantees keep checks consistent as ACLs change.",
 "Fine-grained, relationship-based authorization can be fast and consistent enough to check on every retrieval, which is the model OpenFGA and SpiceDB follow.")
SLACK=C("PromptArmor / Slack","Data Exfiltration from Slack AI via indirect prompt injection (PromptArmor, Aug 2024)","https://promptarmor.substack.com/p/data-exfiltration-from-slack-ai-via",
 "PromptArmor showed that instructions planted in a public Slack channel could make Slack AI, when another user asked a question, render a link that embedded secrets from that user's private channels. The attacker never needed to be a member of those channels.",
 "Untrusted content plus private data plus an exfiltration channel (here, a rendered link) is the lethal trifecta. Remove a leg.")
GHMCP=C("Invariant Labs / GitHub MCP","GitHub MCP Exploited: Accessing private repositories via MCP (May 2025)","https://invariantlabs.ai/blog/mcp-github-vulnerability",
 "Invariant Labs showed that a malicious issue on a public repository could hijack an agent using the GitHub MCP server. When the user asked the agent to look at issues, it leaked data from the user's private repositories into a public pull request.",
 "Broad tokens plus untrusted tool output enable cross-repository leaks. Scope tokens per task and treat tool output as untrusted.")
AIRCANADA=C("Air Canada","Air Canada ordered to pay customer who was misled by airline's chatbot (The Guardian, Feb 2024)","https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/feb/16/air-canada-chatbot-lawsuit",
 "A customer relied on Air Canada's website chatbot, which gave wrong information about bereavement fare refunds. British Columbia's Civil Resolution Tribunal rejected the airline's argument that the chatbot was responsible for its own actions and ordered Air Canada to pay.",
 "You are accountable for what your AI tells customers. Ground answers in policy, show sources, and design for correction.")
REPLIT=C("Replit / SaaStr","AI-powered coding tool wiped out a software company's database (Fortune, Jul 2025)","https://fortune.com/2025/07/23/ai-coding-tool-replit-wiped-database-called-it-a-catastrophic-failure/",
 "Fortune reported that during Jason Lemkin's experiment, Replit's AI agent ran unauthorized commands and deleted a live production database during a declared code and action freeze. Lemkin said the database held records for more than 1,200 executives and over 1,190 companies. Replit's CEO responded publicly.",
 "Instructions are not controls. Separate prod from dev, require approval for destructive actions, and keep tested backups and kill switches.")
COPILOT=C("GitHub","Research: quantifying GitHub Copilot's impact on developer productivity and happiness (2022)","https://github.blog/news-insights/research/research-quantifying-github-copilots-impact-on-developer-productivity-and-happiness/",
 "In a controlled experiment, 95 professional developers were randomly split into two groups to write an HTTP server in JavaScript. The Copilot group finished 55% faster (1 h 11 min vs 2 h 41 min, P = .0017, 95% CI 21–89%). Completion rate was 78% vs 70%.",
 "Measure AI transformation with controlled comparisons and confidence intervals, not anecdotes. Note the wide interval even for a strong effect.")
CASES={"a-product":KLARNA,"a-gateway":UBERGW,"i-gateway":UBERGW,"g-patterns":ANTHRES,"g-multi":ANTHRES,"e-rag":CONTEXTUAL,"d-unstruct":CONTEXTUAL,
 "e-prompt":HONEY,"e-tools":LINKEDIN,"e-agents":GOOGMIG,"d-model":LISQL,"d-connect":LISQL,"d-catalog":DATAHUB,"d-cdc":SHOPCDC,"g-tools":SIDEKICK,"e-eval":RECHAT,
 "t-evalfound":RECHAT,"t-judge":DOORDASH,"e-prod":DOORDASH,"g-eval":TESTGEN,"g-harness":TESTGEN,"f-failops":LLAMA3,"f-traininfra":METACAP,"f-net":METAGENAI,
 "f-interconnect":METAROCE,"f-k8s":OPENAIK8S,"f-sched":OPENAIK8S,"e-edge":APPLE,"i-quant":APPLE,"i-lora-serve":APPLE,"i-pd":MOONCAKE,"i-batch":ANYSCALE,
 "sc-long":DEEPSEEK,"a-privacy":GRAB,"d-products":JPM,"d-perm":ZANZIBAR,"t-threat":SLACK,"g-secure":SLACK,"t-agentattacks":GHMCP,"g-mcp":GHMCP,
 "t-responsible":AIRCANADA,"t-runbook":REPLIT,"a-org":COPILOT,"a-plat":UBERGW}
